/-!
# The free abelian sheaf resolution of a site hypercover

Source: Stacks Project, Hypercoverings, Tags 01GC--01GE and 01GW,
representable case (blueprint: lem-trf-site-hypercover-free-resolution).

The matching-cover condition uses local lifts of compatible proper faces over the
augmentation object. Finite limits identify these families with sections of the
matching objects in the slice. The conclusion concerns the canonical augmentation
of the sheafified free abelian alternating-face complex.
-/

@[expose] public section

set_option autoImplicit false

universe u v

open CategoryTheory CategoryTheory.Limits Opposite
open scoped Simplicial

namespace CategoryTheory

/-- The augmented free abelian sheaf complex of a representable site hypercover is exact.

Source: Stacks Project, Hypercoverings, Tags 01GC--01GE and 01GW,
representable case (blueprint: lem-trf-site-hypercover-free-resolution).

The hypothesis `hcover` is the covering-sieve condition on the canonical matching
maps in `C/W`. A section of a matching object consists of the compatible lower
simplices `p`, all with augmentation `w`. Including noninjective lower operators
adds only the degeneracies forced by the proper faces. At degree zero there are
no lower simplices, and the condition says that `U₀ → W` covers. At degree one it
says that `U₁ → U₀ ×[W] U₀` covers. Pulling back a covering of a matching object
provides the displayed local lifts; conversely, apply `hcover` to the universal
matching family. The covering sieve of lifts then refines the sieve generated by
the matching map. The site axioms identify this with a covering family whose
members factor through that map. No subcanonicality or splitness is required.

The composite of `uliftYoneda`, the sectionwise free abelian functor and
`presheafToSheaf` sends an object `V` to exactly `ℤ[V]`. The standard alternating
augmentation `ε` has that free sheaf on `W`, concentrated in degree zero, as its
target. Thus `QuasiIso` asserts positive homology vanishing and identifies zeroth
homology with `ℤ[W]` through the original augmentation. Equivalently, the displayed
augmented sequence is exact, including surjectivity onto `ℤ[W]`.

Proof for stage 3:
1. Apply Yoneda to `U → W`. Pull back each covering matching map along a section
   of its target. The given factorizations supply local lifts. The sheafification
   universal property and lem-weil-pbc-site-sheaf-sets-epi-locally-surjective show
   that each matching map becomes a locally surjective sheaf map.
2. Form the relative Čech nerve `K⁰` of `h(U₀) → h(W)`, with `(q+1)`-fold fibre
   products over `h(W)`. The degree-zero case of step 1 and
   lem-weil-pbc-cech-nerve-free-homology give zero positive free-sheaf homology and
   the augmentation-induced identification of zeroth homology with `ℤ[W]`.
3. For `n ≥ 1`, form `Kⁿ = coskₙ skₙ h(U)`. By
   lem-weil-pbc-truncated-coskeleton-finite-limits and the coskeleton universal
   property, its degrees through `n` agree with `h(U)`. Restriction and extension
   of compatible cones show that an `m`-coskeleton is `n`-coskeletal for `n ≥ m`.
   The universal property supplies `Kⁿ → Kⁿ⁻¹` for `n ≥ 2`, identical below `n`.
4. By lem-trf-cech-nerve-one-coskeletal, `K⁰` is 1-coskeletal. The common image of
   vertices extends the augmentation of `K¹`; its vertex-tuple map `K¹ → K⁰` is
   the identity in degree zero and the degree-one matching map in degree one.
   Step 1 and lem-weil-pbc-coskeleton-free-homology-comparison at `n = 1` give
   homology isomorphisms in every degree.
5. For `n ≥ 2`, the ordinary presheaf matching limit equals the representable of
   the slice matching object: compatible proper faces have a common image in
   `W`, since their edges equate all vertex augmentations. Forgetting this common
   image and recovering it from a vertex are inverse, natural operations.
   Hence lem-weil-pbc-matching-object-coskeleton identifies `Kⁿ⁻¹ₙ` with that
   matching presheaf and identifies its canonical map from `h(Uₙ)`.
6. Steps 1, 3 and 5 let lem-weil-pbc-coskeleton-free-homology-comparison identify
   the homology of `Kⁿ` and `Kⁿ⁻¹` for `n ≥ 2`. Together with step 4 this gives
   the homology of `K⁰` at every stage. All maps preserve vertex augmentations.
7. For fixed `i`, choose `n ≥ i+1`. The complexes of `h(U)` and `Kⁿ` agree in
   the degrees and differentials computing degree-`i` homology; for `i = 0` only
   degrees zero and one are needed. Stabilization transfers positive vanishing
   and the augmentation-induced zeroth isomorphism from step 2, proving that
   this particular augmentation is a quasi-isomorphism. -/
theorem siteHypercover_free_resolution
    {C : Type u} [Category.{v} C] [HasFiniteLimits C]
    (J : Precoverage C) (hsite : J.IsSiteForCoverings)
    (W : C) (U : SimplicialObject C)
    (e : U ⟶ (Functor.const SimplexCategoryᵒᵖ).obj W)
    (hcover : ∀ (n : ℕ) (T : C) (w : T ⟶ W)
      (p : ∀ (Δ : SimplexCategory), Δ.len < n →
        (Δ ⟶ SimplexCategory.mk n) → (T ⟶ U.obj (op Δ))),
      (∀ (Δ : SimplexCategory) (h : Δ.len < n) (θ : Δ ⟶ SimplexCategory.mk n),
        p Δ h θ ≫ e.app (op Δ) = w) →
      (∀ (Δ Δ' : SimplexCategory) (h : Δ.len < n) (h' : Δ'.len < n)
        (α : Δ' ⟶ Δ) (θ : Δ ⟶ SimplexCategory.mk n),
        p Δ h θ ≫ U.map α.op = p Δ' h' (α ≫ θ)) →
      ∃ S : Sieve T, S ∈ J.toGrothendieck T ∧
        ∀ (V : C) (f : V ⟶ T), S f →
          ∃ t : V ⟶ U _⦋n⦌,
            t ≫ e.app (op (SimplexCategory.mk n)) = f ≫ w ∧
            ∀ (Δ : SimplexCategory) (h : Δ.len < n) (θ : Δ ⟶ SimplexCategory.mk n),
              t ≫ U.map θ.op = f ≫ p Δ h θ)
    : QuasiIso (AlgebraicTopology.AlternatingFaceMapComplex.ε.app
      ((SimplicialObject.Augmented.whiskeringObj C _
        (uliftYoneda.{max u v} ⋙
          (Functor.whiskeringRight Cᵒᵖ (Type (max u v)) AddCommGrpCat.{max u v}).obj
            AddCommGrpCat.free ⋙
          presheafToSheaf J.toGrothendieck AddCommGrpCat.{max u v})).obj
        { left := U, right := W, hom := e })) := by
  sorry

end CategoryTheory
